## Context

Stages 2 and 5 of the pipeline in [[1500]]. Today each extractor is bespoke and
writes its own lines (`build_idn_cipp_priority_projects.py`,
`build_sen_plan_projects.py`, `_vnm_inventory.py`, ...). The core gives them one
way to read a document and one way to write lines, without rewriting the
layout-specific parsers, which are research code and stay as plug-ins.

## Work

- Registry interface: given a `document_id`, return its metadata and the
  bytes of its snapshots by `sha256`. Today it reads `documents.csv`,
  `retrievals.csv` and `data/jetp/documents/objects/`; a later export from a
  document library must swap in without touching extractors.
- Writer: append-only lines under `lines.d/`, `line-field-specs` rows, minted
  `line_id`s `<document_id>-<table>-<ordinal>`, never renumbered.
- Dispositions: a document with no snapshot, a non-canonical duplicate or
  translation, or no extractable content gets a recorded disposition with its
  reason, not silence.
- Idempotence: re-running on an already extracted document changes nothing.
- Build from the first real case (the VNM progress-update series) and
  generalise after two or three cases.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One entry point runs a named document through registry, extractor and writer
- [ ] Re-running on an extracted document leaves the tree byte-identical (test)
- [ ] A missing snapshot, a duplicate and an empty document each yield a disposition (tests)
- [ ] A defect replayed against the writer is rejected (red test): a renumbered `line_id`, a header that disagrees with `line-field-specs`
- [ ] `make jetp-ledger-check`, `make check-fast`, `make lint` and `make check-domain-jetp` pass
